      ":question/id": "7d3bb26e-cfa2-5635-8abf-19fdd50c8376",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 0 & 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that by replacing $a_{41}$, $a_{43}$, and $a_{44}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 0 & 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\longrightarrow\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe nonzero rows have leading entries in columns 1, 2, 4, in strictly increasing order, and all zero rows are at the bottom. The other options will not give us a matrix in row echelon form.",
